const items0 = [["t65z_sp8p1w1p1", () => "Sometimes picturing your little one feels fake. You're sitting there thinking, \"I'm just making this up.\" A real photo of you as a child can help with that, since there's no face or posture to invent. That's you in it, doing whatever you were doing with your hands. Look at it gently, without deciding ahead of time what the picture is supposed to prove, and just see what comes up.", "1e05a1ccdb4ce991b53df513ebd63459f79c84a724345f123196ea1a4c9d7d14"], ["t65z_sp8p4bw1p1", () => "So if a serious accusation starts to take shape, like the idea that someone abused you, don't confront or accuse anyone based only on what came up here. You might catch yourself already writing the message to them in your head. Write down exactly what came up and how it came up, and lean on someone steady who won't lead you toward an answer either way.", "6e0923ac250c5a4204857888108179d0a6c90465cc1e610c4b84e1dd4bd66ded"], ["t65z_ks4bw1p1", () => "But if you're about to bet your life on the opposite claim (that death is simply nothing), please look into it first. Maybe it seems too obvious to need checking. Near-death experiences and the rebirth reports that have been seriously studied can't give you reliable odds about what happens after death. If you read some of them, though, it can get harder to stay certain that destroying the body guarantees nothingness.", "26412d4a7e3e114e0065c2a5a17343306716e8d8d92cbfbc6e4bd95bfa179b14"], ["t65z_lm3w1p1", () => "Rumi said to keep breaking your heart until it stays open. Some people find that deep grieving gives way to warmth or connection. For some it's a feeling in the body, like being held. (That's them describing how it feels. It doesn't prove how the body does that.) Love can open grief from the other side, too. Someone's kind to you and you think, \"Why am I crying?\" Being cared for can bring up sadness for the care that was missing, and that doesn't mean the love failed. Stay with the grief while you're present and can still choose, and when it's swamping you instead of opening you, slow down.", "7fcbe00d2dafb3344dc1139c2d83e6a76ffc7429555c4c1ab7be634cf65a9203"], ["t65z_sp7w1p1", () => "Breathe however you normally do, and after you, the wish takes in anyone else who comes to mind. The idea is you stop carrying a feeling this many people share as proof that you alone are defective. If one of them hurt you, you might wonder if you're supposed to forgive them, or imagine their childhood. It isn't asking you to. You don't contact anyone for this, either. Whether you ever contact them is a separate decision, and never one to make while you're flooded.", "ceb19b60c08d31871a9cbf4ced0dce294734f3e9ea70eb62488292e0155d46e9"], ["t65z_sp5w1p1", () => "Telling it as a story doesn't make it fiction, or prove every detail is right. It ends the trial. Whoever did the harm is still accountable: if it was you, any repair that's owed is still owed, and if someone hurt you, the line doesn't say what they did was okay. Stop if the grandpa voice starts sneering or the shame gets bigger. Same if you start to feel unreal. You might think, \"I'll just tell it from further away, then.\" More distance won't help with those. If the line helps, keep it to just that. Then you can ask what the younger you in that story needs from you now.", "8883a11832492bbe76f94b9220c61b7b77a9e07e3010e7b3a3d0d4076fc8a46e"], ["t65z_sp8p1w2p1", () => "Sometimes picturing your little one feels fake. You're sitting there thinking, \"I'm making this up.\" A real photo of you as a child can help with that, since there's no face or posture to invent. That's what you looked like. Don't decide ahead of time what the picture is supposed to prove, though. Just look at it gently and notice what comes up.", "6aab006458660280dd6fbe18bd33be0e753e15dcfe0f2857a6628fd6bb13fc2c"], ["t65z_sp6w1p1", () => "In the morning, pick one small intention: \"Today I will notice one hook before I act from it.\" You don't have to know yet which hook it'll be. Whichever one shows up will do. At night, look back over the day without putting yourself on trial. Where did you notice the hook? And where did it run its whole course anyway?", "24d4564d4b1e0bddcec7c70d4d29b5a6a85f4fcdf1a4d6f019260881fd4e082b"], ["t65z_sp6w1p2", () => "Maybe you only catch it now, looking back: \"Oh, I got hooked there.\" That's still noticing, and it counts as progress. If you interrupted the pattern somewhere, notice that too. What comes next, a small repair or some way to protect yourself? And what moment did you actually get that felt warm, or that you enjoyed? It might have been a time you were brave, or felt close to someone. Repair whatever can be repaired. Then renew the intention and go to sleep.", "b7d485a6a071d6a24dd1b79629d45a136420976f05b1edb330fecc3a7e9f49ed"], ["t65z_sp9w1p1", () => "Give every session with your little one a definite ending. Look around and name where you are, and feel your feet or the chair, wherever you're touching something. Let your jaw and hands relax. They might have been tight this whole time. Then name one normal thing you'll do next and say something like, \"That's enough for today. We're returning to the life we're building.\" Maybe it doesn't feel like enough. Going back to your life is part of the healing, too, and the session doesn't have to resolve every feeling before you make food or go to sleep.", "9fa692d533b84d0a821e3091f9fded6743753b9b6e66187004843da2f9046764"], ["t65z_sp5w2p1", () => "Telling a memory as a story doesn't turn it into fiction or prove every detail is right. And it only ends the trial. If you did the harm, you're still responsible, and any repair that's owed is still owed. If someone hurt you, the line doesn't say what they did was okay. Stop if the narrator's voice turns sneering or the shame gets bigger. Same if you start to feel unreal. \"I'll tell it from further away, then,\" you might think. More distance won't help with those. If the line does help, keep it to the line. Then you can ask what the younger you in that story needs from you now.", "9fc2a1751fbcf149539df7505c36b6d028bf7c8aeb7e37a846bc6026c67b1ca2"], ["t65z_lm2w1p1", () => "Some people hear all this about love and answer, \"No. I hate myself.\" If that's what you find, don't argue with it. It deserves an honest hearing. You might look inside and think the hate is all there is. But if you opened this article, something in you wanted things to be different. The same goes if you came to therapy or asked someone for help. Or if you'd decided you were hopeless and then tried again anyway.", "0eb81e661805675a71bbde45e44154dc9ada18cbbb3844b56a2671e009f166bb"], ["t65z_lm2w1p2", () => "What was that something in you? Maybe hope, or desperation. It could have been curiosity or fear. Maybe it was duty. Or it was more like, \"I'm not going to keep suffering like this.\" It might even have been love. You don't need to decide which. Ask that part what it hoped might happen when it brought you here, and follow that thread.", "23e6cbf94547fbe1ee1d5f092136ae8a18bb55be3bce70922cadd230dfad73fc"], ["t65z_sp7w2p1", () => "Breathe however you normally do (nothing special). After yourself, include anyone else who comes to mind. If that's someone who hurt you, you might wonder whether you have to forgive them, or imagine their childhood. You don't, and the wish asks for no contact with anyone. Whether you ever contact them is a separate decision, and never one to make while you're flooded. The wish is there so you stop carrying a feeling this many people share as proof that you alone are defective.", "e13bd55c9501988d19848a5ae4753850b38e092ed52593429588c12eada9bc35"], ["t65z_sh1w1p1", () => "Some people have the opposite problem. It feels like you're connecting with your little one, but the contact is staying near the surface. Meditating for a long time and then sleeping can open up much more emotionally direct stuff, especially in a second sleep, after you've already slept once. If that night's dream carries far more feeling and need than the daytime session came anywhere near, you might wake up wondering where all that was. That gap is the sign, and the difference is information about the session (a good session that just didn't carry into your day is something else). Ask what became available, and how you see it now. If repeated nightmares start wrecking your sleep, change the dose before giving up on the whole thing. Try it earlier in the day, or shorter.", "68d01221905215834648741e46377dd01e8fdfabc432787d8d5d6312ea41117e"], ["t65z_lm4w1p1", () => "If you're curious about that bigger happiness, follow the curiosity instead of pretending to believe something. Read people from different traditions, and judge a path by its fruits (what it does for the people on it). Meet people whose lives make you wonder, the kind you walk away from thinking, \"Where does that come from?\"", "64742cb6b1fdc712c944a053500371ee49d60b116f02617ff317757cbf85fade"], ["t65z_lm4w1p2", () => "Sometimes care isn't what's missing. A meal or a boundary can still help, and so can a kind sentence. But when the whole horizon of life feels too small, none of that touches the question underneath: \"Why should I want this life at all?\" You might not have put it in those words. Take that question seriously, though. If being alive feels like something you just endure, what would have to be possible for it to feel worth discovering?", "8120c590e855c63ff96ad1946ab886c02a79a2847b4d7f7ee7dcb4063b27877e"], ["t65z_sp6w2p1", () => "In the morning, choose one modest intention: \"Today I will notice one hook before I act from it.\" Any hook will do, whichever one shows up. At night, go back over the day without putting yourself on trial (yes, that courtroom again). Where did you notice the hook? Where did it run its whole sequence anyway? Some days those turn out to be the same place.", "7f9ba4cba90678bc4598d7bebe863c32394790763abd39d6d0d75cd45ec1c13a"], ["t65z_sp6w2p2", () => "Noticing counts as progress, even if it's only now, looking back, that you think \"oh, that was a hook.\" If you interrupted the pattern somewhere, notice that too. What's the next small repair, or something protective you can do? And what moment of warmth or pleasure did you actually get? Or it might have been a moment of courage, or of connection. Repair what can be repaired and renew the intention, and then go to sleep.", "00996658860e6b5f8ee2210766e818c3fe781b481bbd4b3c2c52cc074770a225"], ["t65z_ba1w1p1", () => "Reparenting is usually talked about as something you do alone, or with a therapist. But your little one got hurt in relationships, and that kind of hurt can only heal so far on your own. Having someone who can care for you when you're struggling can be an enormous blessing. Sometimes one person ends up carrying a lot of the borrowed grown-up jobs at once: food, planning, health decisions, rides, your social life, reassurance, protection, company. Or maybe it's just that feeling of \"okay, I'm with somebody who knows what they're doing.\"", "9334f67be49c01b25625a6a5f266db22b8055bfdc821f58ec25f554a7dbd86df"], ["t65z_ba1w1p2", () => "Over time, reparenting can help you take in some of what that person has modeled for you, and practice it. In a way, they've been showing you how all along. Other jobs can be shared with friends and community, or with professionals or services. Some can go to routines and ordinary tools. Which jobs can become more yours, and which are healthiest shared? The aim is for your little one to feel reliable care through more than one way, while the relationship itself stays precious and important. And which jobs really belong inside that relationship?", "7ab360d453f0fa3202926bb2462472aa3e4e67758bf117e9f3f695d8ac9aafbf"], ["t65z_lm1w1p1", () => "If you weren't loved well as a child, \"love your inner child\" can sound like nonsense. Love them how? Maybe you really want the best for your little one. Maybe you hug them in your imagination, or miss them. And you can still feel none of the warmth people mean when they say love. That doesn't close the door, though, it just means you start smaller.", "1a3a634d51fe82d2b356851290d2dce69af0126d24211f5caee491ebf3f2a24c"], ["t65z_lm1w1p2", () => "Sometimes even an imagined caring grown-up makes you feel more threatened, or lonelier. Part of you might be waiting for the catch. When that happens, go smaller still, to the least cruel response a decent adult could offer your little one. That's scaffolding, and it isn't a test of whether you were loved enough.", "e8e43ecb22dfb76922745b949483166e23fcc9d72aadaa75c079f805926ffe8d"], ["t65z_lm1w1p3", () => "Taking the hostility out makes room for warmth. What people most often say helped is letting their defenses down. I mean by choice, after the part guarding you has been heard. If you've never been safe being loved, your ego can act like a rigid security guard that won't let the feeling in, and being vulnerable is the opening the feeling needs. (This guard is only about letting the feeling in. People still have to earn your trust.) Sometimes the warmth is there, with a cold part standing in front of it, guarding something. That part tends to step aside once it's been heard (see the section Hear the Protective Part First).", "6b592ddfeeff41fc4cc1502b32afe978535fa4bd6e4fc12a2c2c58e9895b8cf0"], ["t65z_le1w1p1", () => "Ask what has ended, and what you're still trying to keep going. What isn't known yet? What is your little one afraid will happen in the gap? Maybe a practical act or small ritual could mark the ending: \"This part is over, and I don't know what comes next.\" When that not-knowing feels unbearable, it's tempting to grab some new identity just so it stops. Don't force one. The grown-up you can keep your routines and protect your body. Grieving what ended is part of it, and so is staying with your little one while the next life takes shape.", "30c9e99ae7b5937e121012e0e51a8fe38275d1b3e029d9b36b87cef8dc23e387"], ["t65z_le1w1p2", () => "Missing someone doesn't have to turn into one more problem to solve. Sometimes the ache itself can be a cue for love, so don't push it away. If love is there, let \"I miss you\" become \"I love you,\" or a simple wish: \"May you be safe. May you be held in love.\" Picture them, or just feel yourself sending it their way. Does any of it reach them? You don't have to believe it does, since this is about what happens in you. It doesn't replace grieving or getting in touch. You'd still keep your boundaries and do whatever practical things need doing. If sending love makes you more compulsive or unreal, make it simpler: \"I miss you, and I care.\" Same if you can't get back to your life afterward. You can also offer some of the same care to the part of you that's still here.", "f07f62f34b0ec7bc235651b211cbaba4fde0eb9af87523e4c031af30f4fbccdc"], ["t65z_sp4w1p1", () => "This is for when talking to your little one, or picturing them, only gets you silence. Write the scared or hurting thoughts and feelings with the hand you don't usually write with (the clumsy one). Its writing already looks like a child's, so that's your little one's hand. Then answer with your usual hand, as the grown-up offering love, protection and guidance. If your little one is still silent, it's tempting to make up what they'd say. But write only what's really there, what you actually feel. The protective part might show up first. Then let it have the clumsy hand until it's said its piece.", "9ccb1c04f9853bc2b328a67739a4ec2e0258e2149644c99a47304d1e525d0ee9"], ["t65z_sp4w1p2", () => "If thinking things through on paper leaves you more confused, a simpler memory aid is fine. It keeps track of things without you having to work out what they mean at the same time. Looking for patterns can come later, if it's useful at all. A memory aid doesn't have to explain your life. It could be a short note of what happened, with the date, or a note of what helped. A reminder or a next step works too. Or you can leave yourself a voice note.", "f3b0402eb0b485fc23d07c8bc28499ebeaa7c6d9f30c8335a6c542b40b518751"], ["t65z_ks12w9p1", () => "If you're about to act, or can't stop yourself, get another person involved before anything else, and put some distance between you and the means. A crisis line or an emergency number counts as a person here. The same goes if you're badly disoriented, or drunk or high, or the means are right in front of you. Not sure whether that's you right now? Then treat it like it is. None of this answers whether your life is worth living. It keeps the question open long enough to look at it.", "5eca392909dce91d1f4a73fdd066f485f2cca9c413267a0760a99a980dc4ba53"], ["t65z_ks12w9p2", () => "Before getting philosophical, check that there's a grown-up in the room. If the part that wants to die feels like all of you, notice who's doing the noticing (Noticing Counts), or borrow an adult, and invite in any part of you that can watch, keep your body safe, or put off a decision you can't undo. Maybe nothing answers yet. Then the questions can wait, and the person from the first step is the adult for now. And if you can already watch the suicidal state without being swallowed by it, skip ahead.", "c915462146bb586d8e90b9d3a032213acd7d65a2fe09a8e993df90479e364610"], ["t65z_sp8p4bw2p1", () => "If you start to suspect someone of something serious, like abusing you, please don't confront or accuse anyone based only on what came up here. You might be looking at the photo thinking, \"That would explain so much.\" Write down exactly what came up and how it came up, and get some support from someone level-headed who won't lead you toward an answer.", "09f3af299a50970a603ce2ab300d36c6eb40cafa2e5b6575fdb2412f7e5023fd"], ["t65z_ks12w1p1", () => "If you're about to act, or can't stop yourself, first get another human being involved, and put distance between you and whatever you'd use. Do that too if you're badly disoriented or intoxicated, or if it's right in front of you. A crisis line or an emergency number counts as another human being. It can be a stranger. If you're reading this and can't tell whether you're about to act, treat it as if you are. This won't answer whether life is worth living. It keeps the question open long enough to look into it.", "dedb306d083c848e748b74da1d2f38203adf2ae34b008eccb4e9046038f4296d"], ["t65z_ks12w1p2", () => "Before getting philosophical, make sure there's an adult in the room. If the part that wants to die feels like all of you, notice who's doing the noticing (Noticing Counts) or borrow an adult, whichever's easier, and invite any part that can watch what's happening or protect your body. Maybe one can put off a decision you can't undo. Skip all that if you're already able to watch the suicidal state without being completely caught up in it. If no part of you can watch yet, the questions wait, and the person you got involved is the adult for now.", "5fa66810b714a02dbc5d18d1d3c9556f730eb5e8efda81354cfd401bdfe43756"], ["t65z_lm2w2p1", () => "Some people hear all this about love and answer, \"No. I hate myself.\" If that's what you find, it deserves an honest hearing. Just listen to it, without arguing back. But if you opened this article, something in you wanted things to be different, whatever the rest of you was saying. The same goes if you came to therapy or asked someone for help. Or maybe you'd already decided you were hopeless, and then for some reason you tried again.", "df90a74228c8e416a8b347ab806c9bd288d9947a00ac6510072e5df78ecaefa3"], ["t65z_lm2w2p2", () => "What was it? It might have been hope, or desperation. Maybe you were curious, or scared. Some people come here out of duty. Or it was more like, \"I'm not going to keep suffering like this.\" And maybe it was love. You don't need to decide which. Ask that part what it hoped might happen when it brought you here, and follow that thread.", "596b6841e94ffa9112d46e76738a8c5b35d9e6c200fb11729fd5074d5e4838b5"], ["t65z_sp4w2p1", () => "This one's for when talking to your little one, or picturing them, only gets you silence. Write the scared or hurting thoughts and feelings with the hand you don't usually write with, the clumsy one. Its writing already looks like a child's, which is why it's your little one's hand. Then answer with your usual hand, as the grown-up offering love, protection and guidance. If the clumsy hand just hovers, you might start thinking up what they'd say. Only write what's really there, though, what you actually feel. And if the protective part shows up first, let it use the clumsy hand until it's said all it wants to.", "9b8a35bfd05badec45e3cb4ffff41182e3921bd1c74f50eaa19807703ae7a534"], ["t65z_sp4w2p2", () => "If journaling to make sense of things leaves you more confused, a simpler memory aid is fine. That could be a short note with the date and what happened, or a note of what helped. A reminder or a next step works too. So does a voice note. Maybe you still catch yourself wondering what it all means. A memory aid keeps track of things without you having to work that out at the same time, and it doesn't have to explain your life. Looking for patterns can come later, if it's useful at all.", "b4dee52374bfab8740cdd39198d63966efd36df23199611ddb3d9997bb08ff9a"], ["t65z_wl1w1p1", () => "Be patient with yourself, and gentle, including with the part of you that thinks you should be over it by now. You are more than your pain and your past. That's easy to lose sight of when you've spent so long looking at them. And you aren't alone. There's help, and lots of other people are out there learning to look after their own little ones.", "cf57eb7759cf71887837d4e08211a751edad62aa36ef5665fcd20430e2cccb7a"], ["t65z_lm1w2p1", () => "If you weren't loved well as a child, \"love your inner child\" can sound like nonsense. Maybe you sincerely want the best for your little one. You might hug them in your imagination, or miss them. And you could still feel none of the warmth people mean when they say love, and end up wondering what everyone else is talking about. The door's still open, though. You'd be starting smaller.", "3f44ea2a79f9b067232958d0326eff25427492be7ccf59d8fdbd1170eb56266c"], ["t65z_lm1w2p2", () => "If just imagining a caring grown-up makes you feel more threatened, or lonelier, go smaller still. This is all scaffolding, and nobody's using it to test whether you were loved enough. Think of the least cruel response a decent adult could offer. Someone who doesn't love your little one could manage that much.", "20dfe71ed0e0149b952a396f83dee45678f62f169b7c8808aa42e83118d5e376"], ["t65z_lm1w2p3", () => "Taking the hostility out makes room for warmth. What people most often say helped is letting their defenses down, which might sound scary. You'd do it by choice, though, once the part of you that's on guard has been heard. That's different from pushing past it. If you've never been safe being loved, your ego can act like a rigid security guard that won't let that feeling in, and being vulnerable is the opening it needs. That guard is only about letting the feeling in. People still have to earn your trust. Sometimes the warmth isn't missing. A cold part is standing in front of it, guarding something, and that part tends to step aside once it's been heard.", "806afd49a02508427d7863f684467058f7006b651b9ace5dbf0fa516ea927d1a"], ["t65z_lm3w2p1", () => "Rumi says you have to keep breaking your heart until it stays open. Some people find that deep grieving gives way to warmth, or to feeling connected. Some feel held, in the body. That describes what they feel, and it doesn't prove how the body produces it. Being loved can open grief from the other side, too. Someone takes care of you, and you think, \"Why does this make me sad?\" It can be sadness for the care that was missing, and that doesn't mean the love failed. Stay with the grief while you're present and can still choose, and when it's swamping you instead of opening you, slow down.", "7adf375991393b5e3ce1624a51234fe4cee61a4c5cce53cb8a6a2a7a5d4529f1"], ["t65z_lm4w2p1", () => "If you're curious about that bigger happiness, follow the curiosity instead of pretending to believe something. Read people from different traditions, and meet people whose lives make you wonder. You might already know someone like that, who's happy in a way you can't quite figure out. And judge a path by its fruits: what are the people who follow it like?", "b3fc124254840a6b12f435108148a0ab10cc855fc8c39a0399939a8bf6c91540"], ["t65z_lm4w2p2", () => "Sometimes the whole horizon of life feels too small. Care isn't what's missing then. A meal can help, or a boundary, or a kind sentence, without touching the question underneath: \"Why should I want this life at all?\" Take that question seriously. Maybe right now, life is something you're just enduring. What would have to be possible for being alive to feel worth discovering?", "be2b7a78133191bc327cc56647df7e4307af7c574d049d366e8f90e5c81bc57a"], ["t65z_sp9w2p1", () => "Every session with your little one needs a definite ending, photo or not. Look around and name where you are. Feel where you're touching something, like the floor under your feet or the chair, and relax your jaw and hands. Name one normal thing you'll do next, whatever that is, and say something like \"That's enough for today. We're returning to the life we're building.\" Maybe it feels like you two were just getting somewhere. Going back to your life is part of the healing, too. The session doesn't have to resolve every feeling before you make food or go to sleep.", "e328ea6b938995a633fc4adb1025cde3ae5ea0a9a9c724a4874d8130975b5bb8"], ["t65z_secba1w1", () => "# Borrowed Adulthood in Relationship\n\nReparenting is usually talked about as something you do alone, or with a therapist. But your little one got hurt in relationships, and that kind of hurt can only heal so far on your own. Having someone who can care for you when you're struggling can be an enormous blessing. Sometimes one person ends up carrying a lot of the borrowed grown-up jobs at once: food, planning, health decisions, rides, your social life, reassurance, protection, company. Or maybe it's just that feeling of \"okay, I'm with somebody who knows what they're doing.\"\n\nOver time, reparenting can help you take in some of what that person has modeled for you, and practice it. In a way, they've been showing you how all along. Other jobs can be shared with friends and community, or with professionals or services. Some can go to routines and ordinary tools. Which jobs can become more yours, and which are healthiest shared? The aim is for your little one to feel reliable care through more than one way, while the relationship itself stays precious and important. And which jobs really belong inside that relationship?", "d3096282bc94f2192740cc42f3f20c97bc73996b882ca840e2d3dd98b2d34d3d"], ["t65z_secle1w1", () => "# When One Life Has Ended and the Next Hasn\u2019t Begun\n\nYour little one can get really loud in the gap after one life ends and before the next one has formed. A breakup will do it, or moving to another country, an illness, losing your faith or a role, your youngest moving out. So can leaving a family or community, or staying in one on new terms. Tibetan Buddhism calls an in-between state a bardo. You don't have to buy what it teaches about death and rebirth to use the everyday part: endings leave a stretch where the old you is gone and the new one hasn't shown up.\n\nAsk what has ended, and what you're still trying to keep going. What isn't known yet? What is your little one afraid will happen in the gap? Maybe a practical act or small ritual could mark the ending: \"This part is over, and I don't know what comes next.\" When that not-knowing feels unbearable, it's tempting to grab some new identity just so it stops. Don't force one. The grown-up you can keep your routines and protect your body. Grieving what ended is part of it, and so is staying with your little one while the next life takes shape.\n\nMissing someone doesn't have to turn into one more problem to solve. Sometimes the ache itself can be a cue for love, so don't push it away. If love is there, let \"I miss you\" become \"I love you,\" or a simple wish: \"May you be safe. May you be held in love.\" Picture them, or just feel yourself sending it their way. Does any of it reach them? You don't have to believe it does, since this is about what happens in you. It doesn't replace grieving or getting in touch. You'd still keep your boundaries and do whatever practical things need doing. If sending love makes you more compulsive or unreal, make it simpler: \"I miss you, and I care.\" Same if you can't get back to your life afterward. You can also offer some of the same care to the part of you that's still here.", "9e12ab1d9531d04044a296a69751beafdf3b169015f1baf878784bf0804d9e3f"], ["t65z_seclm1w1", () => "# When Love Still Feels Missing\n\nIf you weren't loved well as a child, \"love your inner child\" can sound like nonsense. Love them how? Maybe you really want the best for your little one. Maybe you hug them in your imagination, or miss them. And you can still feel none of the warmth people mean when they say love. That doesn't close the door, though, it just means you start smaller.\n\nSometimes even an imagined caring grown-up makes you feel more threatened, or lonelier. Part of you might be waiting for the catch. When that happens, go smaller still, to the least cruel response a decent adult could offer your little one. That's scaffolding, and it isn't a test of whether you were loved enough.\n\nTaking the hostility out makes room for warmth. What people most often say helped is letting their defenses down. I mean by choice, after the part guarding you has been heard. If you've never been safe being loved, your ego can act like a rigid security guard that won't let the feeling in, and being vulnerable is the opening the feeling needs. (This guard is only about letting the feeling in. People still have to earn your trust.) Sometimes the warmth is there, with a cold part standing in front of it, guarding something. That part tends to step aside once it's been heard (see the section Hear the Protective Part First).", "f3f8e316a5f93ab1739504325cec81e32fe1f2f76c62d6fdc9463071c336d318"], ["t65z_seclm1w2", () => "# When Love Still Feels Missing\n\nIf you weren't loved well as a child, \"love your inner child\" can sound like nonsense. Maybe you sincerely want the best for your little one. You might hug them in your imagination, or miss them. And you could still feel none of the warmth people mean when they say love, and end up wondering what everyone else is talking about. The door's still open, though. You'd be starting smaller.\n\nIf just imagining a caring grown-up makes you feel more threatened, or lonelier, go smaller still. This is all scaffolding, and nobody's using it to test whether you were loved enough. Think of the least cruel response a decent adult could offer. Someone who doesn't love your little one could manage that much.\n\nTaking the hostility out makes room for warmth. What people most often say helped is letting their defenses down, which might sound scary. You'd do it by choice, though, once the part of you that's on guard has been heard. That's different from pushing past it. If you've never been safe being loved, your ego can act like a rigid security guard that won't let that feeling in, and being vulnerable is the opening it needs. That guard is only about letting the feeling in. People still have to earn your trust. Sometimes the warmth isn't missing. A cold part is standing in front of it, guarding something, and that part tends to step aside once it's been heard.", "b8040a9cc58fc2e35c0c0b447a5415e8f5e24d16e64efd7718b53cad1e1bafa9"], ["t65z_seclm2w1", () => "## Notice the Part That Came Here\n\nSome people hear all this about love and answer, \"No. I hate myself.\" If that's what you find, don't argue with it. It deserves an honest hearing. You might look inside and think the hate is all there is. But if you opened this article, something in you wanted things to be different. The same goes if you came to therapy or asked someone for help. Or if you'd decided you were hopeless and then tried again anyway.\n\nWhat was that something in you? Maybe hope, or desperation. It could have been curiosity or fear. Maybe it was duty. Or it was more like, \"I'm not going to keep suffering like this.\" It might even have been love. You don't need to decide which. Ask that part what it hoped might happen when it brought you here, and follow that thread.", "b93b6168ad11c9d61ce0e48a8e399b285925b76494b081a07c03125c03b8fd09"], ["t65z_seclm2w2", () => "## Notice the Part That Came Here\n\nSome people hear all this about love and answer, \"No. I hate myself.\" If that's what you find, it deserves an honest hearing. Just listen to it, without arguing back. But if you opened this article, something in you wanted things to be different, whatever the rest of you was saying. The same goes if you came to therapy or asked someone for help. Or maybe you'd already decided you were hopeless, and then for some reason you tried again.\n\nWhat was it? It might have been hope, or desperation. Maybe you were curious, or scared. Some people come here out of duty. Or it was more like, \"I'm not going to keep suffering like this.\" And maybe it was love. You don't need to decide which. Ask that part what it hoped might happen when it brought you here, and follow that thread.", "f06e6254cc25522497b4fce1efe2f74db535725e81f8c3d6b96e7ecc5ef11171"], ["t65z_seclm3w1", () => "## Somatic Heart Opening\n\nRumi said to keep breaking your heart until it stays open. Some people find that deep grieving gives way to warmth or connection. For some it's a feeling in the body, like being held. (That's them describing how it feels. It doesn't prove how the body does that.) Love can open grief from the other side, too. Someone's kind to you and you think, \"Why am I crying?\" Being cared for can bring up sadness for the care that was missing, and that doesn't mean the love failed. Stay with the grief while you're present and can still choose, and when it's swamping you instead of opening you, slow down.", "c2caedfbb3cc35911188e330095a511c73121d8c5b11d6883d74bab32bba24df"], ["t65z_seclm3w2", () => "## Somatic Heart Opening\n\nRumi says you have to keep breaking your heart until it stays open. Some people find that deep grieving gives way to warmth, or to feeling connected. Some feel held, in the body. That describes what they feel, and it doesn't prove how the body produces it. Being loved can open grief from the other side, too. Someone takes care of you, and you think, \"Why does this make me sad?\" It can be sadness for the care that was missing, and that doesn't mean the love failed. Stay with the grief while you're present and can still choose, and when it's swamping you instead of opening you, slow down.", "de153da04f0ec1cb2641768193cc13d24b8e26e87fbc1a27a7b23886fde61302"], ["t65z_seclm4w1", () => "## How Much Happiness Do You Know Is Possible?\n\nPart of love is wanting someone to be well, and how far that wish can reach depends partly on what kind of happiness you know is possible. If the happiness you know is food, safety, affection, friends, work that means something and some help when you need it, then wanting those things for somebody is love. Babies get some of this already. Practical love matters a lot.\n\nBut a big spiritual experience can show you a kind of peace, or a love, you didn't know existed, and then \"I want you to be happy\" suddenly means something much bigger. That's partly why loving-kindness meditation can sound almost comically simple to someone who's never been near what it's pointing at, and so powerful to someone who has. Saying \"may you be happy\" over and over isn't automatically some profound love. It's one way of finding that deeper love and growing it.\n\nIf you're curious about that bigger happiness, follow the curiosity instead of pretending to believe something. Read people from different traditions, and judge a path by its fruits (what it does for the people on it). Meet people whose lives make you wonder, the kind you walk away from thinking, \"Where does that come from?\"\n\nSometimes care isn't what's missing. A meal or a boundary can still help, and so can a kind sentence. But when the whole horizon of life feels too small, none of that touches the question underneath: \"Why should I want this life at all?\" You might not have put it in those words. Take that question seriously, though. If being alive feels like something you just endure, what would have to be possible for it to feel worth discovering?\n\nCuriosity can be enough here, something like \"Other people say they've felt a depth of love or peace that I never have. I don't know if I believe their explanation. But what if the experience is possible for me?\" Don't assume the limits of what you've felt so far are the limits of what's possible.", "3fa0b6e9e143abafa488f787df9375b5a041f07125d8c996046d4feb0125e03f"], ["t65z_seclm4w2", () => "## How Much Happiness Do You Know Is Possible?\n\nPart of love is wanting someone to be well, and how far that wish can reach depends partly on what kind of happiness you know is possible. If the happiness you know is food, safety, affection, friends, work that means something and some help when you need it, then wanting those things for somebody is love. Babies get some of this already. Practical love matters a lot.\n\nBut a big spiritual experience can show you a kind of peace, or a love, you didn't know existed, and then \"I want you to be happy\" suddenly means something much bigger. That's partly why loving-kindness meditation can sound almost comically simple to someone who's never been near what it's pointing at, and so powerful to someone who has. Saying \"may you be happy\" over and over isn't automatically some profound love. It's one way of finding that deeper love and growing it.\n\nIf you're curious about that bigger happiness, follow the curiosity instead of pretending to believe something. Read people from different traditions, and meet people whose lives make you wonder. You might already know someone like that, who's happy in a way you can't quite figure out. And judge a path by its fruits: what are the people who follow it like?\n\nSometimes the whole horizon of life feels too small. Care isn't what's missing then. A meal can help, or a boundary, or a kind sentence, without touching the question underneath: \"Why should I want this life at all?\" Take that question seriously. Maybe right now, life is something you're just enduring. What would have to be possible for being alive to feel worth discovering?\n\nCuriosity can be enough here, something like \"Other people say they've felt a depth of love or peace that I never have. I don't know if I believe their explanation. But what if the experience is possible for me?\" Don't assume the limits of what you've felt so far are the limits of what's possible.", "581dbcc844d8c36052ca75a49858f94fdb3e5f9647ebb373ce3ef0e560531ac0"], ["t65z_secsh1w1", () => "# When the Practice Feels Real but Stays Shallow\n\nSome people have the opposite problem. It feels like you're connecting with your little one, but the contact is staying near the surface. Meditating for a long time and then sleeping can open up much more emotionally direct stuff, especially in a second sleep, after you've already slept once. If that night's dream carries far more feeling and need than the daytime session came anywhere near, you might wake up wondering where all that was. That gap is the sign, and the difference is information about the session (a good session that just didn't carry into your day is something else). Ask what became available, and how you see it now. If repeated nightmares start wrecking your sleep, change the dose before giving up on the whole thing. Try it earlier in the day, or shorter.", "de1a7d1b6f3a34e6d5e966983b42833d42c1b761604800865565343a95f55ee2"], ["t65z_secsp4w1", () => "## Journal With Each Hand\n\nThis is for when talking to your little one, or picturing them, only gets you silence. Write the scared or hurting thoughts and feelings with the hand you don't usually write with (the clumsy one). Its writing already looks like a child's, so that's your little one's hand. Then answer with your usual hand, as the grown-up offering love, protection and guidance. If your little one is still silent, it's tempting to make up what they'd say. But write only what's really there, what you actually feel. The protective part might show up first. Then let it have the clumsy hand until it's said its piece.\n\nIf thinking things through on paper leaves you more confused, a simpler memory aid is fine. It keeps track of things without you having to work out what they mean at the same time. Looking for patterns can come later, if it's useful at all. A memory aid doesn't have to explain your life. It could be a short note of what happened, with the date, or a note of what helped. A reminder or a next step works too. Or you can leave yourself a voice note.", "87aad34734eaca399f2b21771e02765d818201b4317c97e83573048e8519a644"], ["t65z_secsp4w2", () => "## Journal With Each Hand\n\nThis one's for when talking to your little one, or picturing them, only gets you silence. Write the scared or hurting thoughts and feelings with the hand you don't usually write with, the clumsy one. Its writing already looks like a child's, which is why it's your little one's hand. Then answer with your usual hand, as the grown-up offering love, protection and guidance. If the clumsy hand just hovers, you might start thinking up what they'd say. Only write what's really there, though, what you actually feel. And if the protective part shows up first, let it use the clumsy hand until it's said all it wants to.\n\nIf journaling to make sense of things leaves you more confused, a simpler memory aid is fine. That could be a short note with the date and what happened, or a note of what helped. A reminder or a next step works too. So does a voice note. Maybe you still catch yourself wondering what it all means. A memory aid keeps track of things without you having to work that out at the same time, and it doesn't have to explain your life. Looking for patterns can come later, if it's useful at all.", "920d1659844f1e11e168bb4f8e5eff4775f1d6785b64aeaf0b8369d81f1f4690"], ["t65z_secsp5w1", () => "## \u201cAnd So That\u2019s the Way It Happened!\u201d\n\nFinish a hard memory with \"And so that's the way it happened!\" the way a grandpa would finish a story on the porch. (\"Once upon a time\u2026\" helps if the start is where you get stuck.) Somehow it moves the whole thing out of the courtroom, where the case was still being argued, and into your life story. Pick a memory you can handle for the first few tries.\n\nTelling it as a story doesn't make it fiction, or prove every detail is right. It ends the trial. Whoever did the harm is still accountable: if it was you, any repair that's owed is still owed, and if someone hurt you, the line doesn't say what they did was okay. Stop if the grandpa voice starts sneering or the shame gets bigger. Same if you start to feel unreal. You might think, \"I'll just tell it from further away, then.\" More distance won't help with those. If the line helps, keep it to just that. Then you can ask what the younger you in that story needs from you now.", "32e9a37722889da7fc5fdbfbb726843701dc0eb20d5fc56ffab1b75c28cfd4af"], ["t65z_secsp5w2", () => "## \u201cAnd So That\u2019s the Way It Happened!\u201d\n\nFinish a hard memory with \"And so that's the way it happened!\" the way a grandpa would finish a story on the porch. (\"Once upon a time\u2026\" helps if the start is where you get stuck.) Somehow it moves the whole thing out of the courtroom, where the case was still being argued, and into your life story. Pick a memory you can handle for the first few tries.\n\nTelling a memory as a story doesn't turn it into fiction or prove every detail is right. And it only ends the trial. If you did the harm, you're still responsible, and any repair that's owed is still owed. If someone hurt you, the line doesn't say what they did was okay. Stop if the narrator's voice turns sneering or the shame gets bigger. Same if you start to feel unreal. \"I'll tell it from further away, then,\" you might think. More distance won't help with those. If the line does help, keep it to the line. Then you can ask what the younger you in that story needs from you now.", "b95bd64c214f597fbdc3c0b20d5e5a6c39f8cc58c6d78be72b8eb246e6871212"], ["t65z_secsp6w1", () => "## One Intention in the Morning, One Review at Night\n\nIn the morning, pick one small intention: \"Today I will notice one hook before I act from it.\" You don't have to know yet which hook it'll be. Whichever one shows up will do. At night, look back over the day without putting yourself on trial. Where did you notice the hook? And where did it run its whole course anyway?\n\nMaybe you only catch it now, looking back: \"Oh, I got hooked there.\" That's still noticing, and it counts as progress. If you interrupted the pattern somewhere, notice that too. What comes next, a small repair or some way to protect yourself? And what moment did you actually get that felt warm, or that you enjoyed? It might have been a time you were brave, or felt close to someone. Repair whatever can be repaired. Then renew the intention and go to sleep.", "c269bee993c1667c0e296590de413773d1d578272ebac9465d85c9fe47388a90"], ["t65z_secsp6w2", () => "## One Intention in the Morning, One Review at Night\n\nIn the morning, choose one modest intention: \"Today I will notice one hook before I act from it.\" Any hook will do, whichever one shows up. At night, go back over the day without putting yourself on trial (yes, that courtroom again). Where did you notice the hook? Where did it run its whole sequence anyway? Some days those turn out to be the same place.\n\nNoticing counts as progress, even if it's only now, looking back, that you think \"oh, that was a hook.\" If you interrupted the pattern somewhere, notice that too. What's the next small repair, or something protective you can do? And what moment of warmth or pleasure did you actually get? Or it might have been a moment of courage, or of connection. Repair what can be repaired and renew the intention, and then go to sleep.", "aa0e789638cd9949404679de6bb686bc5864b2d280c6a4b51a907c5b0328e3a3"], ["t65z_secsp7w1", () => "## Just Like Me\n\nThis one works best on a steadier day, when thinking about other people won't pull you out of your own shoes. Name the feeling plainly. Lonely, say. Then think of how many people are feeling that exact thing right now, and wanting it to stop, just like you. Some of them make it worse trying to get away from it. Just like you, sometimes. \ud83d\ude05 Wish all of you, you first, enough support to stay with it, so the pain stops with you.\n\nBreathe however you normally do, and after you, the wish takes in anyone else who comes to mind. The idea is you stop carrying a feeling this many people share as proof that you alone are defective. If one of them hurt you, you might wonder if you're supposed to forgive them, or imagine their childhood. It isn't asking you to. You don't contact anyone for this, either. Whether you ever contact them is a separate decision, and never one to make while you're flooded.", "0605784b8357d55c90327e62e302618d5fb56bb5fbca0b232e0daa676276ab1d"], ["t65z_secsp7w2", () => "## Just Like Me\n\nThis one works best on a steadier day, when thinking about other people won't pull you out of your own shoes. Name the feeling plainly. Lonely, say. Then think of how many people are feeling that exact thing right now, and wanting it to stop, just like you. Some of them make it worse trying to get away from it. Just like you, sometimes. \ud83d\ude05 Wish all of you, you first, enough support to stay with it, so the pain stops with you.\n\nBreathe however you normally do (nothing special). After yourself, include anyone else who comes to mind. If that's someone who hurt you, you might wonder whether you have to forgive them, or imagine their childhood. You don't, and the wish asks for no contact with anyone. Whether you ever contact them is a separate decision, and never one to make while you're flooded. The wish is there so you stop carrying a feeling this many people share as proof that you alone are defective.", "e755c973161d782e6f014eaed77ebb9c91a53f4ab81405274cd45b3b867ff8e9"], ["t65z_secsp9w1", () => "## Close the Session and Return to Life\n\nGive every session with your little one a definite ending. Look around and name where you are, and feel your feet or the chair, wherever you're touching something. Let your jaw and hands relax. They might have been tight this whole time. Then name one normal thing you'll do next and say something like, \"That's enough for today. We're returning to the life we're building.\" Maybe it doesn't feel like enough. Going back to your life is part of the healing, too, and the session doesn't have to resolve every feeling before you make food or go to sleep.", "729300998acad014e4db8967518f95e3df411ff367304b69f634ac8c36cc3679"], ["t65z_secsp9w2", () => "## Close the Session and Return to Life\n\nEvery session with your little one needs a definite ending, photo or not. Look around and name where you are. Feel where you're touching something, like the floor under your feet or the chair, and relax your jaw and hands. Name one normal thing you'll do next, whatever that is, and say something like \"That's enough for today. We're returning to the life we're building.\" Maybe it feels like you two were just getting somewhere. Going back to your life is part of the healing, too. The session doesn't have to resolve every feeling before you make food or go to sleep.", "6f0acccf49da8b13b6f84e1c6aa6857e087eabb703820e5a3681c794ddcd0c0b"]];
const rev = !!window.__reverse;
const items = rev ? items0.slice().reverse() : items0;
const order0 = items0.map(x => x[0]);
const lane = k => "t65z" + '_' + k;
try { localStorage.removeItem(lane(rev ? 'rev' : 'fwd')); } catch (e) {}
window.__t65z = [];
const log = m => { window.__t65z.push(m); try { localStorage.setItem(lane(rev ? 'rev' : 'fwd'), JSON.stringify(window.__t65z)); } catch (e) {} };
const other = () => { try { return JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(lane(rev ? 'fwd' : 'rev')) || '[]').filter(m => / (clicked|taking)$/.test(m)).map(m => order0.indexOf(m.split(':')[0])); } catch (e) { return []; } };
const setter = Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(HTMLTextAreaElement.prototype, 'value').set;
const sha = async t => Array.from(new Uint8Array(await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', new TextEncoder().encode(t)))).map(b => b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join('');
const button = () => [...document.querySelectorAll('button[type=submit]')].find(b => /check for ai/i.test(b.innerText));
const wait = ms => new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, ms));
async function run() {
  for (const [k, mk, want] of items) {
    const o = other(), i = order0.indexOf(k);
    if (o.length && (rev ? i <= Math.max(...o) : i >= Math.min(...o))) { log('stop at ' + k + ': the other tab has the rest'); break; }
    log(k + ': taking');  // claimed before the work, so the other tab never takes it too (turn 65: with a margin
                          // of one instead, both tabs stopped short of the last check)
    const T = mk();  // built when its turn comes, so it can use texts stored earlier in this batch
    for (let j = 0; j < 20 && !(button() && document.querySelector('textarea')); j++) await wait(500);
    const ta = document.querySelector('textarea');
    if (!ta) { log(k + ': no textarea'); break; }
    setter.call(ta, T); ta.dispatchEvent(new Event('input', {bubbles: true}));
    let b = null;
    for (let j = 0; j < 30; j++) { await wait(500); b = button(); if (b && !b.disabled) break; }
    const h = await sha(ta.value);
    if (h !== want) { log(k + ': hash mismatch ' + h + ' (is its prefix in sessionStorage?)'); break; }
    if (!b || b.disabled) { log(k + ': button not ready'); break; }
    try { sessionStorage.setItem(k, T); } catch (e) {}
    b.click(); log(k + ': clicked');
    await wait(10000);
  }
  log('end');
}
run(); 'started'
